  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TZ CWAF_Talent is equally Distributed</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">While the creative industries, particularly the arts and fashion, have recently offered significant job opportunities and created safe spaces for creative expression, the available opportunities are not uniformly dispersed and shared. Furthermore, religion is frequently perceived as a continuance of patriarchy, whereas feminism is seen as excluding males from the discussion. As a result, our project intends&#xa0;to bring together and focus attention on feminist and faith-based artists, curators, authors, art critics, faith leaders, teachers, and others from diverse creative backgrounds in order to promote and facilitate national dialogue aimed at promotingwomen entrepreneurship in the creative sector through a focus on fashion creativity. Support the establishment of an art and fashion studio, capacity building, and the organization of art exhibitions and fashion events to promote women&apos;s empowerment in the creative industry.

The established art and fashion studio/or hub will provide a chance for female artists and fashion designers to use the space and resources available for the production of their art and design for fashion for economic empowerment. The knowledge gained during the capacity building program will assist them in expressing themselves to the community through art and fashion, with the goal of closing the economic equality gap between men and women in the creative business.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Culture and Women Advancement Found</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Culture and Women Advancement Foundation (CWAF) is a well-known Tanzanian NonGovernmental Organization (NGO). It was legally registered in Tanzania on April 24, 2017, under the registration number 00NGO/0009166. The founders founded the organization after realizing the relevance of peace and security to the growth of residents in any country. The founders also recognized that no society can alleviate poverty if its population lack constitutional rights, good government, and democracy.

The purpose of CWAF is to provide people development by encouraging public participation in economic development and developing an economy for them. The group also strives to mediate problems between communities, promote culture and arts as a technique for poverty reduction, and encourage collaboration and economic development among Tanzanians. The Culture and Women Advancement Foundation&apos;s mission is to have and see a society free of poverty and economic development. Transparency, accountability, trustworthiness, honesty, and equality are the most crucial ideals of the Culture and Women Advancement Foundation.

Since its inception in 2017, the Culture and Women Advancement Foundation has been assisting local communities in Tanzania in addressing poverty and violence against women and girls. However, the organizationis encountering financial constraints that limit the organization&apos;s ability to carry out the tasks. The most serious barrier that the semi-urban and rural poor have in engaging in productive economic activity is a lack of access to micro-loans. Households with handicapped, widowed, or chronically ill members are particularly sensitive to social and economic shocks. They are all unserved by banks since they lack the collateral required for a bank loan. As aresult, they must rely on various sources of informal loans at exorbitant interest rates, trapping them in vicious debt cycles. To address the aforementioned issue, the Culture and Women Advancement Foundation rallied women and youth to join economic groups called Women Revolving Loan Fund (WRLF). The establishment of WRLF is in the works, and it is expected to provide soft micro-loans and training to marginalized and poor populations in Tanzania&apos;s semi-urbanand rural areas, allowing them to engage in meaningful social and economic activities.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
